    tags: ["L5"],
    relatedModuleIds: [],
    relatedProgramIds: ["steadier-sleep"],
    signoffRowIds: ["CV10_A10"],
    body: "## The idea\nStress and sleep feed each other. A body on alert finds it harder to fall and stay asleep, and poor sleep makes it harder to cope with stress the next day. Bad dreams and waking in the night are common after stressful times.\n\n## Why habits help\nSleep isn't something you can force. Trying hard to sleep usually keeps you more awake. What you can do is set things up so sleep is more likely: a steady getting-up time, a wind-down routine, and a bed your body links with rest rather than lying awake.\n\n## What to expect\nChanges to sleep habits often feel harder before they feel easier, and improvements tend to be gradual. A bad night doesn't undo your progress.\n\n## A small practice\nPick one wind-down habit for tonight. Just one.\n\nIf you snore loudly, stop breathing in your sleep, or feel sleepy while driving, please talk to a doctor. Those are worth checking.",
